{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U8jNw6Vgv6xD5UMYynyyCR","type":"tool_result","content":"# loop0014 — pathwidth after ETH: items\n\nOpenAI's family 378 (*The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False*, 2026-10-25,\nLean-verified against a comparator statement) gives a deterministic\n`#k`-SAT algorithm subexponential in the **number of variables**. Pathwidth\nis a permutation problem whose natural CNF encodings use `Θ(n²)` variables,\nso the theorem says nothing about it directly, and every \"no `2^{o(n)}`\nunless ETH fails\" statement about width parameters is void if the theorem\nstands. The plan, `reports/subexp_pathwidth_plan.md`, fixes the premise as a\nnamed `Prop` never asserted (§0.4.1), linearity in vertices as the only\ntransfer (§0.4.2), classes before the general case (§0.4.3), no compute on\ninstances (§0.4.4), four subagents per session at most (§0.4.5), and brute\nforce to 7 vertices before any statement (§0.4.6). Kill criteria are in\n§0.6; item 01 can kill the loop.\n\nThe report is `reports/subexp_pathwidth.md`; scratch under\n`reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/<item>/`; Lean under\n`lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/` (imported by\n`lean/MOSPFormalization.lean`).\n\n- [x] **01 The audit and the kill check: exact bases, ETH-conditional lower bounds, and what the counting method needs from a problem.**\n  `pdftotext` the ETH paper once; read Sections 1, 2 (technique overview),\n  6 (the reduction of `#k`-SAT to counting common zeros) and Appendix D\n  (tensor contraction), and skim 3–5 for the objects (shared linear\n  summaries, low-degree replacement forms, sparse polynomial systems).\n  Write `reports/subexp_pathwidth.md` §1 with: (a) the best published exact\n  algorithms for pathwidth / vertex separation, treewidth, bandwidth and\n  cutwidth with their bases and citations (held papers under `literature/`\n  first; Suchan & Villanger, Kitsunai et al., Bodlaender–Fomin–Koster–\n  Kratsch–Thilikos, Fomin & Villanger, Cygan & Pilipczuk for bandwidth);\n  (b) every ETH- or SETH-conditional lower bound on those parameters we can\n  find in held papers or in the citation graph, each with the reduction it\n  rests on and the reduction's vertex blow-up; (c) **the two-page account\n  of what the counting method needs from a problem, in our words**: the\n  input form (variables per object, clause width, sparsity), where the\n  `2^{n/√log n}` comes from, what breaks if the constraint width is\n  unbounded, and whether the method is a counting method only or decides\n  structured existence too. Then the kill check of plan §0.6: stop the\n  loop (mark 02–09 `[!]`, leave 10–11) if a `2^{o(n)}` exact pathwidth\n  algorithm is on record or the method demonstrably cannot apply to any\n  NP-hard width problem. One subagent at most. Ends with \"what this does\n  not claim\".\n\n- [x] **02 The formal frame: `SubexpCount`, an algorithm-with-time-bound notion, and `PathwidthSubexp`.**\n  In `lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean`: decide, and\n  record in the report, whether to copy OpenAI's `PrimitiveMachine` model\n  (`lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean`, 220 lines over\n  Mathlib's `Turing.TM0`; Apache-2.0, verbatim with attribution in the file\n  header and a `literature/MISSING.md` entry) or to use an abstract notion\n  (a decidable predicate with a time function, `∃ T : ℕ → ℕ, (∀ ε > 0,\n  eventually T n ≤ 2^(ε n)) ∧ …`). Define `SubexpCount (k : ℕ) : Prop`\n  restating `exact_count_and_sat` (never proved, never an axiom: a `def`\n  of a `Prop`), `PathwidthSubexp : Prop` (\"some machine decides `pw G ≤ k`\n  for every `n`-vertex `G` and `k` in time `2^{o(n)} · poly`\"), and the\n  class-restricted `PathwidthSubexpOn (C : SimpleGraph → Prop)`. Prove the\n  two trivial lemmas (monotonicity in the class; `PathwidthSubexp ↔\n  PathwidthSubexpOn (fun _ => True)`). `lake build`; axiom audit; no\n  `sorry`.\n\n- [x] **03 The reduction catalogue: every reduction into pathwidth or vertex separation on record, with its blow-up; the best linear `X`.**\n  From held papers and the citation graph: Lengauer 1981, Kashiwabara &\n  Fujisawa 1979 (missing: say so), Arnborg–Corneil–Proskurowski 1987\n  (treewidth via cobipartite graphs), Monien & Sudborough (cutwidth,\n  vertex separation on planar/degree-3 graphs), Bodlaender's and\n  Gustedt's hardness proofs, Linhares & Yanasse 2002 (MOSP), Kinnersley\n  1992, the `Complex/` chain. For each: source problem, target problem,\n  vertex blow-up as a function of the source size, and whether the\n  source problem is itself believed to lack `2^{o(N)}` algorithms after\n  ETH. Write §3 with the table and name the best `X` with a **linear**\n  reduction, or state that none is on record. Every reduction claimed\n  linear is implemented in `learning/subexp_reductions.py` and checked on\n  every graph (or source instance) to 7 vertices against brute-force\n  vertex separation; log under `reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/03_reductions/`.\n\n- [x] **04 Classes with `O(n)`-bit layouts: cobipartite, split, bounded-degree, co-comparability, the paper-2 MOSP cover classes.**\n  For each class where pathwidth (or treewidth = pathwidth) is NP-hard:\n  is a layout, or the decision \"`vs ≤ k`\", determined by `O(n)` Boolean\n  variables under constraints of bounded width in the sense of item 01's\n  account? Cobipartite first (Arnborg et al.: `tw = pw` and NP-hard;\n  what does a layout look like, and is the decision a cut condition on one\n  side?); then split graphs, graphs of maximum degree 3 (pw NP-hard,\n  Monien & Sudborough), co-comparability graphs, and the clique-tree\n  covers of paper 2. Write §4 with one subsection per class ending in\n  \"fits / does not fit, because …\" and a brute-force check to 7 vertices\n  of any claimed encoding. If none fits, say so and mark the positive\n  track as the chain-count attempt only (plan §0.6).\n\n- [x] **05 The chain count as an algebraic object.**\n  The number of feasible closing orders is `Σ_σ Π_t [S_t(σ) ∈ F_k]`; the\n  decision is whether it is positive. Ask whether this sum is the number\n  of common zeros of a sparse polynomial system in `o(n²)` variables in\n  the sense of item 01's account: write the threshold constraint\n  `|N(S) ∖ S| ≤ k − 1` as a polynomial over a field, measure its degree and\n  sparsity, and name the obstacle (unbounded width, or the ordering\n  variables) if it does not fit. Test any claimed encoding by counting on\n  every graph to 7 vertices (`learning/two_ended.py` and\n  `itertools.permutations`) and log under\n  `reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/05_chain/`. Write §5 ending in a\n  verdict for plan §0.6's chain-count kill.\n\n- [x] **06 Algorithms from the counting theorem for problems on graphs of linear pathwidth: the crossover, as algorithms with proofs.**\n  For every problem with a published `c^{pw} · poly` or `c^{tw} · poly`\n  algorithm given a decomposition (independent set `2`, `q`-colouring `q`,\n  dominating set `3`, Cut & Count's connectivity problems, SAT by primal\n  pathwidth; take the list from Lokshtanov–Marx–Saurabh 2011, Cygan et al.\n  2011/2022, Lampis 2025, each cited), ask whether the problem has a\n  bounded-width CNF with `O(n)` variables in the sense of item 01's\n  account (one variable per vertex; dominating set only at bounded degree;\n  exact Max-Cut and Max-`k`-SAT through the weighted count if §1.3 says the\n  method supports `Q[y]/(y^ℓ)` weights — check the paper's Appendix C\n  first and cite it where it already says so). For each that does, state\n  **the algorithm** (encode, run the counting machine, read the count) with\n  **a proof of its running time** `2^{O(n/√log n)} · poly` (paper form) and\n  the verified form, and **the crossover proposition**: it beats the\n  decomposition DP once `pw > n / (log₂ c · √log n)` up to the constants,\n  tabulated for `c = 2, 3, 4` at `n = 10²…10⁶`, with the classes on the far\n  side named (linear pathwidth: expanders, `G(n, c/n)` by our `β*`, the\n  Chu & Stuckey ridge). Beside each: the SETH-tight lower bound it\n  previously carried, now void. Lean (`SubexpPathwidth/Crossover.lean`):\n  the crossover inequality as a proposition over the frame of item 02,\n  and `SubexpCount k → ` the problem's decision in the stated time for at\n  least independent set, with the encoding's correctness proved. Brute\n  force to 7 vertices for every encoding. Write §6.\n\n- [ ] **07 New exact algorithms by exact linear transformation, unconditional.**\n  (a) The converse of item 03's expansion: vertex separation of a graph of\n  maximum degree 3 as the cutwidth of a linear-size graph (or a direct\n  argument), so that the published `O(1.89ⁿ)` pathwidth algorithm\n  (Kitsunai et al., held) becomes an exact cutwidth algorithm on subcubic\n  graphs with base below 2 — where Bentert et al. 2025 state no base below\n  2 is known; if the transformation costs a factor `α` in the vertex\n  count the base is `1.89^α` and is reported honestly (and compared with\n  `2`). (b) Item 04's algorithms stated as algorithms with proofs and\n  implemented in `learning/subexp_algorithms.py` with tests: cobipartite\n  vertex separation in `O*(2^{max(a, b)})`, vertex separation in `O*(2^u)`\n  over twin classes (the equivalent-customers reduction of MOSP with a\n  proof), starlike chordal pathwidth in `2^{O(√n)}` for centre `≤ √n`.\n  (c) Any further exact transformation at blow-up `≤ 2` found between\n  pathwidth and a `Complex/` problem that lacks a base below 2. Every\n  algorithm run against brute force on all graphs to 7 vertices and on\n  the 149 cobipartite and 252 subcubic atlas graphs. Write §7.\n\n- [ ] **08 Lean for item 07: the transformations' width equivalences and the algorithms' correctness.**\n  `SubexpPathwidth/Transform.lean`: the converse transformation of 07(a)\n  as a definition and `cw(T(G)) = vs(G)` (or the inequality pair actually\n  proved) against `VertexSeparation.lean` and a cutwidth definition stated\n  here; the cobipartite normal form (Theorem 4.1) and the twin lemma\n  (Theorem 4.4) as theorems, since they are what 07(b)'s algorithms rest\n  on. No `sorry`, no `axiom`; axiom audit; a `decide` check on one small\n  instance where feasible. Every statement brute-force checked to 7\n  vertices before it is stated.\n\n- [ ] **09 The pathwidth complex after ETH: an algorithm with a proof for every Table 1 problem.**\n  For every problem of Linhares & Yanasse (2002) Table 1 proved equivalent\n  in `lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/` (gate matrix layout, interval\n  thickness, node search, narrowness, one-dimensional logic, MOSP, …) and\n  for the sandwiched ones (split bandwidth, edge search): the best exact\n  algorithm **now**, as a theorem — `O(1.89ⁿ)` carried through each\n  blow-up-1 equivalence (cite the Lean equivalence it rides on, state the\n  parameter: `n` customers for MOSP, vertices for the graph problems),\n  the sandwiched ones with the base the sandwich allows, cutwidth-like\n  ones through item 07 — and, where a problem has a bounded-width\n  `O(n)`-variable encoding (none of the ordering problems; say so per\n  problem), the counting theorem's time beside it. Then every ETH/SETH\n  statement about a complex member (held papers, the citation graph),\n  void. The deliverable is a table `problem → algorithm → time → proof\n  (Lean name or section)`, with a paragraph per row; prose that is not\n  attached to an algorithm or a theorem is cut. Write §9.\n\n- [ ] **10 What fell, and what survives: the maps.**\n  (a) every published statement that computing pathwidth, treewidth,\n  vertex separation, bandwidth, cutwidth, MOSP or a `Complex/` problem\n  needs `2^{Ω(n)}` or `2^{Ω(√n)}` under ETH or SETH (held papers first;\n  `paper1/citation_graph.py` for the rest): the sentence, the reduction,\n  the status now. (b) The surviving hypotheses (`P ≠ NP`;\n  `NP ⊄ DTIME(2^{o(N)})` in input length; Set Cover, Orthogonal Vectors,\n  3-SUM, APSP — which stand on their own once SETH is gone) and, for each,\n  whether a `2^{o(n)}` pathwidth algorithm at `k = Θ(n)` would touch it;\n  FPT settles `k = o(n^{1/2})` (TASK.md). (c) Confirm nothing in\n  `paper1/`, `paper2/` or `reports/` rests on ETH. Short, as a reference\n  section for items 06 and 09, which carry the mathematics. Write §10.\n\n- [ ] **11 The write-up: `reports/subexp_pathwidth.md` §0, the note, the CLAUDE.md paragraph (drafted, not applied).**\n  §0 in two pages, in the stance of TASK.md: the algorithms this loop\n  produced with their running times and their Lean names first; the\n  crossover; what is unconditional and what is `SubexpCount →`; the\n  question at `k = Θ(n)` as it now stands; what is not claimed. A note\n  *Pathwidth after ETH* as `paper3/pathwidth_after_eth.md` (prose only)\n  organised by algorithm, not by map. `literature/MISSING.md` entries for\n  every paper cited but not held. The CLAUDE.md paragraph in the report's\n  last section, **not applied**. Run the full gate.\n\n- [ ] **12 Reserve.** The algebraic question item 05 leaves sharpest —\n  whether a sum of products over a cube counts width-`k` layouts of a\n  class (the cobipartite cut condition, the twin-class structure) in the\n  paper's framework, as an algorithm with a proof or a stated obstacle,\n  one session with its own kill; or whichever of 06–09 ended short.\n----\n# Pathwidth after ETH: a subexponential exact algorithm, or what its absence would cost — plan for Ralph loop0014\n\n*Written 2026-11-04, after the checkpoint relaxation and the two-ended\nmeasurement (`reports/checkpoint_relaxation.md`) closed the last cheap\nroutes to the 125-vertex refutations. Nothing here has been run. The loop\ndirectory would be `Ralph_Loops/loop0014/` (`TASK.md`, `iterations.md`,\n`PROGRESS.md`, `loop.py`, `gate.py`, `knobs.json`, `allowed_sorries.txt`),\ncloned from loop0013's. Not launched.*\n\n## 0. The question, the hypothesis it rests on, and why now\n\n### 0.1 The landscape changed on 2026-10-25\n\nOpenAI's manuscript collection (`github.com/openai/math`, Apache-2.0;\ncloned read-only under `~/dev/untrusted/openai-math`, never built or run\nfrom inside the repository) contains family 378, *The Exponential Time\nHypothesis is False*: a deterministic algorithm that counts the satisfying\nassignments of any `k`-CNF on `n` variables in time\n`(n + m)^{O_k(1)} · 2^{O_k(n (log log n)² / √log n)}` and polynomial space.\nThe Lean library behind it is 3,977 files, no `sorry`, the three standard\naxioms, and the comparator statement\n(`lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean`,\n`exact_count_and_sat`) is a concrete multi-tape machine with an explicit\nruntime bound in the *number of variables*. We take no position on whether\nthe community will accept it; everything this loop proves is stated\n**conditionally on a named `Prop` that restates that theorem and is never\nasserted**, exactly as loop0012 handled Fomin–Høie's `CubicPathwidthBound`.\nIf the result stands, SETH falls with it (SETH ⇒ ETH), and every\n\"no `2^{o(n)}` algorithm unless ETH fails\" statement in the width\nliterature is void.\n\n### 0.2 The question\n\nVertex separation — hence pathwidth, hence MOSP — is a *permutation*\nproblem: its witness is an ordering of `n` vertices, its natural exact\nalgorithm the `O*(2^n)` subset DP, and the best published base is below 2\nbut not `2^{o(n)}`. The ETH paper's Corollary 1.3 gives `2^{o(v)}` for\nproblems with **one Boolean variable per vertex and bounded-width\nconstraints** (independent sets, `q`-colourings for fixed `q`); it says\nnothing about permutation problems, and the natural CNF encodings of\n\"`pw ≤ k`\" use `Θ(n²)` variables, on which the theorem is worse than the\nDP. So, after ETH:\n\n> **Q.** Does pathwidth admit a `2^{o(n)}` exact algorithm on `n`-vertex\n> graphs, unconditionally or conditionally on the subexponential counting\n> theorem? And if not: which surviving hypothesis would a `2^{o(n)}`\n> algorithm for pathwidth refute, through a reduction that is **linear** in\n> the number of vertices?\n\nEither answer is a clean statement. A positive answer on any class where\npathwidth is NP-hard is a headline; a negative answer that names the\nhypothesis and formalises the reduction is a note that the literature\nneeds the morning ETH falls. **What this loop does not promise** is a\npractical solver: `2^{n/√log n}` at `n = 125` with unknown constants is not\na run, and no item spends compute on an open instance.\n\n### 0.3 What is on record (nothing to solve twice)\n\n- `reports/checkpoint_relaxation.md` §10: the decision \"`MOSP ≤ k`\" is\n  reachability inside `F_k = {S : |N(S) ∖ S| ≤ k − 1}`; the forward\n  reachable lattice is within 2.3× of the search's node count at 30–50;\n  `R_b(n − j) = C(n, j)` for `j ≤ k − 1`. The subset DP *is* the lattice,\n  and the search is near its floor. A `2^{o(n)}` algorithm must not\n  enumerate it.\n- `learning/two_ended.py` enumerates `R_f(i)` exactly to a cap: the\n  instrument for item 06 (how `|F_k|` and `|R_f|` grow with `n` on\n  bounded-degree graphs at `k = pw`).\n- `lean/MOSPFormalization/`: vertex separation, pathwidth, their equality,\n  `MOSP = pw + 1`, treewidth, the sandwich, all `sorry`-free: the\n  definitions any reduction theorem is stated against.\n- `paper1/`, `paper2/`, `reports/`: **no reliance on ETH or SETH**\n  (checked 2026-11-04, zero files). Nothing of ours needs revision.\n- OpenAI's `Computation.PrimitiveMachine` model (220 lines, self-contained\n  over Mathlib's `Turing.TM0`): a formal notion of \"algorithm with a time\n  bound\" that makes `∃ machine, time ≤ f(n)` a `Prop`. Reusable under\n  Apache-2.0 with attribution; item 02 decides whether to copy it or to\n  state algorithms abstractly.\n\n### 0.4 Design decisions (fixed)\n\n1. **The hypothesis is a `Prop`, never an axiom.** `SubexpCount k : Prop`\n   restates `exact_count_and_sat` in our own file; theorems read\n   `SubexpCount k → …`. `allowed_sorries.txt` stays empty; the gate counts\n   axioms.\n2. **Linear means linear in vertices.** A reduction from problem `X` on\n   size-`N` instances to pathwidth on `O(N)` vertices transfers\n   `2^{o(n)}`; a quadratic one transfers nothing. Every reduction in the\n   catalogue (item 03) carries its blow-up, and only linear ones enter a\n   theorem.\n3. **Classes before the general case.** The positive track (item 04) looks\n   for a class where pathwidth is NP-hard *and* a layout is determined by\n   `O(n)` bits under bounded-width local constraints. A positive result on\n   such a class is worth more than a failed attempt on all graphs.\n4. **No compute on open instances; measurement on generated graphs only**\n   (item 06, random cubic and random `G(n, c/n)` at `n ≤ 60`, seconds each).\n5. **Subagents: at most 4 per session**, stated in `TASK.md` (loop0013's\n   item 07 fanned out per class; its cost was first recorded as $947 and\n   is $38 by the corrected reading of the session logs, 2026-11-04).\n6. **Every mathematical claim gets a small-instance check before it is\n   stated** (the loop0004 lesson): a reduction is tested on every graph to\n   7 vertices against brute-force vertex separation before Lean sees it.\n7. **Reading the ETH paper is an item, not a background task.** Its\n   Sections 2–6 (technique overview, shared linear summaries, replacement\n   forms, common zeros of sparse systems, the reduction) are 40 pages; item\n   01 reads them and writes a two-page account of *what the method needs\n   from a problem* in our words, which every later item cites instead of\n   the paper.\n\n### 0.5 Items, one line each, in dependency order\n\n| # | item | depends on | kind | cap |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 01 | The audit: best exact bases for pw/tw/vs/bandwidth/cutwidth; every ETH/SETH-conditional lower bound on them, with its reduction and blow-up; what the counting theorem's method needs from a problem (two pages, our words); the kill check | — | reading, writing | 4 h |\n| 02 | The formal frame: `SubexpCount k` restated; an algorithm-with-time-bound notion (copy of the `PrimitiveMachine` model with attribution, or an abstract one, decided here); `PathwidthSubexp : Prop`; `lake build` | 01 | Lean | 3 h |\n| 03 | The reduction catalogue: every linear-in-vertices reduction *into* pathwidth / vertex separation on record, and the best `X` such that `PathwidthSubexp → X ∈ 2^{o(N)}`; each candidate checked on all graphs to 7 vertices | 01 | reading, code | 4 h |\n| 04 | Classes with `O(n)`-bit layouts: cobipartite (tw = pw, NP-hard), split, bounded-degree, co-comparability, the MOSP cover classes of paper 2; for each, whether \"`vs ≤ k`\" is a bounded-width constraint system in `O(n)` Boolean variables | 01 | mathematics | 4 h |\n| 05 | The chain count as an algebraic object: `#` feasible chains as a sum over orderings; whether it is the number of common zeros of a sparse polynomial system in `o(n²)` variables; the obstacle named if not | 01, 04 | mathematics | 4 h |\n| 06 | Measurement: `|F_k|` and `|R_f|` at `k = pw` against `n` on random cubic graphs and `G(n, 3/n)` to `n = 60` (`learning/two_ended.py`); the exponential rate; is the reachable lattice ever `2^{o(n)}` on a class where pw is NP-hard? | — | compute (minutes) | 2 h |\n| 07 | The reduction theorem, on paper: `PathwidthSubexp → X ∈ 2^{o(N)}` for item 03's `X`, with the reduction's correctness proved; if item 03 finds only quadratic reductions, the theorem is \"no linear reduction into pathwidth is on record\" and item 07 tries to build one from Hamiltonian path or bandwidth | 03 | mathematics | 4 h |\n| 08 | The reduction theorem, in Lean: the graph construction and the width equivalence (`vs(G') ≤ k ↔ X(I)`), stated against `VertexSeparation.lean`, `sorry`-free | 07 | Lean | 4 h |\n| 09 | The positive attempt: if item 04 found a class with `O(n)`-bit layouts and bounded-width constraints, write the encoding and prove `SubexpCount k → pathwidth on the class ∈ 2^{o(n)}`; else the attempt on the chain count (item 05's route) with a stated kill | 04, 05 | mathematics, Lean | 4 h |\n| 10 | The hypothesis map: which published lower bounds on width parameters rested on ETH or SETH (with citations and the exact statement voided), and which hypotheses survive (`P ≠ NP`, `NP ⊄ SUBEXP` in input length, the Set Cover and Orthogonal Vectors conjectures' status); a table for the note | 01 | reading, writing | 3 h |\n| 11 | The write-up: `reports/subexp_pathwidth.md` (§0 two pages), the note *Pathwidth after ETH* in `paper1/latex/` or a new `paper3/` stub, the CLAUDE.md paragraph, `literature/MISSING.md` entries | all | writing | 3 h |\n| 12 | Reserve: whichever of 07–09 ended short, or an independent re-check of item 08's statement by a refuter agent against brute force | 11 | — | 4 h |\n\nTwelve items; Lean items at loop0012's rate (6–20 min each) and reading\nitems at the cap; **estimate 12–20 hours wall, $300–450** at loop0012–13's\ncost per item with the subagent cap honoured. **The owner's budget is\n$2,500 (set 2026-11-04 after launch, raised from $1,500)**: the difference over the estimate is\nfor the two hard-mathematics items (07, the linear construction; 09, the\npositive attempt) to take a second session each through the reserve, and\nfor a refuter agent on every Lean statement, not for more fan-out.\n\n### 0.6 Kill criteria, stated up front\n\n- **Item 01 kills the loop** if a `2^{o(n)}` exact algorithm for\n  pathwidth is already on record, or if the counting theorem's method\n  demonstrably needs `O(n)` variables in a way no width problem can\n  supply and no NP-hard class supplies either. Then items 10 and 11 run\n  (the note is still worth having) and 02–09 are marked `[skipped]`.\n- **Item 04 kills the positive track** (09 becomes the chain-count attempt\n  only) if no NP-hard class has `O(n)`-bit layouts under bounded-width\n  constraints.\n- **Item 05 kills the chain-count route** if the obstacle is structural\n  (threshold constraints of unbounded width are inherent), stated in a\n  paragraph with the smallest example.\n- **Item 03 lowers item 07's ambition** if every known reduction into\n  pathwidth is superlinear: the theorem becomes the absence statement plus\n  one attempted construction, not a transfer theorem.\n- **Budget**: **$2,500 total** (owner, 2026-11-04; first $1,500, raised the same evening with the re-plan to algorithms with proofs). The coordinator sums the\n  `# result` lines of the session logs after each iteration and sets\n  `knobs.json` `stop` when the sum passes $2,500 or any session exceeds\n  its cap; the driver re-reads the knobs before each session.\n\n### 0.7 The three biggest risks\n\n1. **The ETH paper is wrong, or its method is narrower than its abstract.**\n   Mitigated by decision 1: nothing here asserts it, and item 01's two-page\n   account is what the rest of the loop uses. If the method needs more\n   than \"one variable per vertex, bounded-width clauses\", item 04's class\n   search uses the true requirement.\n2. **The positive track produces nothing** (the likely outcome). Then the\n   loop's product is the reduction theorem of 07–08 and the hypothesis map\n   of item 10 — a publishable note either way, and the first formal\n   statement of what a subexponential pathwidth algorithm would refute.\n3. **Reductions into pathwidth are all quadratic** (the classical ones go\n   through cutwidth or interval completion with size blow-up). Then the\n   negative side has no transfer theorem and item 07's attempt at a linear\n   construction is the hard mathematics of the loop; its kill is one\n   session.\n\n### 0.8 Gate\n\n`gate.py` from loop0013 with: `lake build` of `lean/`, `sorry` count against\nan empty `allowed_sorries.txt`, axiom audit of every new theorem\n(`paper1/axiom_check.lean` pattern, extended to the new files), pytest on\n`tests/test_two_ended.py`, `tests/test_checkpoint_bound.py` and any new\ntest module with a 900 s timeout, and a check that no file under\n`solutions/` or `reports/scratch/obstruction_search/` changed.\n\n## 1. What this plan does not claim\n\nIt does not claim a solver, a bound, or a refutation of anything on the\nrecord. It does not assert the ETH result. It does not re-run any proved\ninstance. It claims, at the end, one of: a conditional `2^{o(n)}` algorithm\nfor pathwidth on a named NP-hard class, formalised; or a formalised\nreduction theorem naming what a `2^{o(n)}` pathwidth algorithm would refute\nand a note placing pathwidth in the post-ETH landscape.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"09aaa989-9eda-444c-a4fd-1cc6d69ace0c","uuid":"5cd8feec-5de7-40a0-80df-5b1d431aecf1","timestamp":"2026-11-05T00:05:17.076Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# loop0014 — pathwidth after ETH: items\n\nOpenAI's family 378 (*The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False*, 2026-10-25,\nLean-verified against a comparator statement) gives a deterministic\n`#k`-SAT algorithm subexponential in the **number of variables**. Pathwidth\nis a permutation problem whose natural CNF encodings use `Θ(n²)` variables,\nso the theorem says nothing about it directly, and every \"no `2^{o(n)}`\nunless ETH fails\" statement about width parameters is void if the theorem\nstands. The plan, `reports/subexp_pathwidth_plan.md`, fixes the premise as a\nnamed `Prop` never asserted (§0.4.1), linearity in vertices as the only\ntransfer (§0.4.2), classes before the general case (§0.4.3), no compute on\ninstances (§0.4.4), four subagents per session at most (§0.4.5), and brute\nforce to 7 vertices before any statement (§0.4.6). Kill criteria are in\n§0.6; item 01 can kill the loop.\n\nThe report is `reports/subexp_pathwidth.md`; scratch under\n`reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/<item>/`; Lean under\n`lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/` (imported by\n`lean/MOSPFormalization.lean`).\n\n- [x] **01 The audit and the kill check: exact bases, ETH-conditional lower bounds, and what the count