{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U1scjqYiGFvzRfjs1uyvRp","type":"tool_result","content":"treewidth and treedepth, there are algorithms that run in O ∗ (1.73461n ) time [10] and\nin O ∗ (1.9602n ) time [8], respectively, although no algorithm is known for cutwidth\nwhose running time is O ∗ (cn ) for some c < 2.\n\n                                                                               123\n\f140                                                          Algorithmica (2016) 75:138–157\n\n\n   For the computation of pathwidth, Suchan and Villanger [24] improved the running\ntime to O(1.9657n ) and also gave an additive constant approximation of pathwidth in\nO(1.89n ) time. On the other hand, no algorithm faster than O ∗ (2n ) time was known\nfor directed pathwidth before the present work.\n   Our result is as follows.\n\nTheorem 1 The directed pathwidth of a digraph with n vertices can be computed in\nO(1.89n ) time.\n\n    Our algorithm can be viewed as one based on Bellman-Held-Karp style dynamic\nprogramming. For each U ⊆ V (G), let N − (U ) denote the set of in-neighbors of\nU . Given a positive integer k and a digraph G, we build the collection of “feasible”\nsubsets of V (G), where U ⊆ V (G) is considered feasible if G[U ∪ N − (U )] has a\ndirected path-decomposition of width at most k whose last subset X t contains N − (U ).\nTo get a non-trivial bound on the number of subsets U to be considered, we adopt\na strategy essentially due to Suchan and Villanger [24]. Since either U , N − (U ), or\nV (G)\\(U ∪ N − (U )) has cardinality at most n/3, the hope is that we may be able\nto obtain a non-trivial upper bound on the number of relevant subsets U using the\nwell-known upper bound 2 H (α)n on the number of subsets of V (G) with cardinality at\nmost αn, where H (x) is the binary entropy function. Setting α = 13 , this upper bound\nis O(1.89n ) since 2 H (1/3) < 1.89. The difficulty, as observed in [24], is that there\nmay be an exponential number of subsets U with N − (U ) = S for each S. The larger\nconstant 1.9567 or the relaxation to additive constant approximation in [24] comes\nfrom the need to deal with this problem.\n    A key to overcoming this difficulty is the following observation whose undirected\nversion is used in [24]. Suppose we are to decide whether the directed pathwidth of G\nis at most k and S is a vertex set of G with |S| ≤ k −g, where g is some positive integer.\nLet C be the set of strongly connected components of G[V (G)\\S] with cardinality\n===== BENTERT\n19:                                               Arc Set, Optimal Linear Arrangement, Cutwidth, and Pathwidth. Most of our al-\n61:     Directed Cutwidth                                          2               O∗ (2 3 )\n95:Arc Set, Optimal Linear Arrangement, Cutwidth, and Pathwidth. For all of the above\n98:Arc Set or Cutwidth can be solved faster than O∗ (2n ) have been explicit open problems for\n164:   In the problem Directed Cutwidth2 , we are looking for an ordering that minimizes the\n168:Cutwidth. An alternative definition takes a directed acyclic graph as input and searches for a topological ordering\n169:of the vertices minimizing the (undirected) cutwidth.\n175:\f       Directed Cutwidth\n181:cardinality. Directed Cutwidth can be solved in O(2n n2 ) time and the weighted version can be\n426:\fFigure 3: An example of Directed Cutwidth. The input instance is depicted on the left\n431:Directed Cutwidth are depicted. The ordering shown on the top has a directed cutwidth of 3\n432:(e.g. at positions 4 and 5) and the ordering on the bottom has a directed cutwidth of 2.\n462:3.2    Cutwidth\n463:We continue with Directed Cutwidth. We again show how to use Proposition 1 to compute a\n466:Proposition 2. Directed Cutwidth can be 2-approximated in O∗ (2 3 ) time.\n467:Proof. Let G = (V, A) be an input to Directed Cutwidth. We first find an optimal solution L to\n473:is by definition a lower bound f