the directed cutwidth of G. Next, we can solve G[L] and G[R]\n476:directed cutwidth, we have that the cutwidth of G[L] and G[R] are both at most the cutwidth of G.\n479:that the cutwidth of G′ is at most the cutwidth of G. See Figure 3 for an example. Adding the\n480:set A∗ to G′ can increase the cutwidth by at most |A∗ | and adding arcs (u, v) with u ∈ L and v ∈ R\n481:does not increase the directed cutwidth. Thus, the computed ordering is a 2-approximation as\n482:the size of A∗ is at most the cutwidth of G as shown above. Note that the running time is\n489:\f   Unfortunately, due to the inherent maxima nature of Directed Cutwidth, we do not know\n627:Feedback Arc Set, Directed Optimal Linear Arrangement, and Directed Cutwidth\n765:4.2    Cutwidth and Optimal Linear Arrangement\n766:In this final part, we generalize our algorithms for Directed Cutwidth and Directed Optimal\n768:structure, we only show the result for Directed Cutwidth.\n769:Proposition 7. Weighted Directed Cutwidth admits a (2 + ε)-approximation in time\n773:instance to Weighted Directed Cutwidth. We find a (1 + ε)-approximation for the in-\n780:a lower bound for the weighted directed cutwidth of G. Next, we can solve G[L] and G[R] opti-\n783:its cutwidth, we have that the weighted directed cutwidth of G[L] and G[R] are both at most\n784:the weighted directed cutwidth of G. Adding the edges from L to R also does not increase the\n785:cutwidth. Hence, placing all vertices in L according to the computing solution for G[L] and then\n787:that shows that the weighted directed cutwidth of G′ is at most the weighted directed cutwidth\n788:of G. Adding the set A′ to G′ can increase the cutwidth by at most w(A′ ) and hence the computed\n855: [5] Nikhil Bansal, Dor Katzelnick, and Roy Schwartz. On approximating cutwidth and pathwidth.\n882:     On cutwidth parameterized by vertex cover. Algorithmica, 68(4):940–953, 2014.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_