ions.py && echo ===== CLASSES && sed -n 50,130p learning/subexp_classes.py && sed -n 230,300p learning/subexp_classes.py && echo ===== CW && sed -n 300,340p paper1/complex_check.py && echo ===== MISSING && tail -30 literature/MISSING.md && grep -n -i 'expansion' paper1/equivalences.md | head","description":"Fetch Kitsunai's undirected remark, Bentert's open-problem sentence, reusable code, MISSING.md format"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01USpZpFYBoHBzm9gi4PUcSo":{"cwd":"/home/al/dev/MOSP"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01USpZpFYBoHBzm9gi4PUcSo","type":"tool_result","content":"23:undirected graph in the same amount of time, improving on the algorithm due to\n31:The pathwidth [2,21] of an undirected graph G is defined as follows. A path-\n62:The directed pathwidth of G is defined similarly to the pathwidth of an undirected\n65:    For an undirected graph G, let Ĝ denote the digraph obtained from G by replacing\n70:pathwidth of an undirected graph is a special case of the problem of computing the\n95:    Without parameterization, both directed and undirected pathwidth can be solved in\n98:problems (see [4]). Similarly, some undirected graph parameters related to pathwidth,\n132:    A key to overcoming this difficulty is the following observation whose undirected\n215:[26] (see also [15] for the undirected case). Based on this fact, we work on the directed\n274:   Following the terminology of [24] for the undirected case, we call U ⊆ V (G) a full\n319:tions of the lemma, U ∪ C is strongly k-feasible if U is. The undirected counterpart\n=====\nproblems in the works of Esmer et al. [15, 16, 17]. Even more recently, Inamdar et al. [26] gave\na framework for designing exponential-time approximation schemes for graph partitioning (i.e.,\ncut) problems and a few more examples for specific problems are known [8, 9, 4]. However, none\nof these techniques seem applicable to vertex-ordering problems, where the objective is to find an\nordering of vertices that satisfies some polynomial-time satisfiable predicate, such as Feedback\nArc Set, Optimal Linear Arrangement, Cutwidth, and Pathwidth. For all of the above\nexcept Pathwidth, the O∗ (2n )-time dynamic programming remains the best known algorithm–\neven including constant-factor approximations. Moreover, the question whether e.g., Feedback\nArc Set or Cutwidth can be solved faster than O∗ (2n ) have been explicit open problems for\na long time [21, 24, 25, 13]. This reinforces our belief that it is time to study exponential-time\napproximation algorithms for vertex-ordering problems. The only example of such a study is in\n===== CODE\n\nOUT = Path(\"reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/03_reductions\")\nINF = 10 ** 9\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# exact evaluators\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef vs_subset_dp(adj) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Vertex separation by DP over prefix sets, vectorised by popcount layer:\n    `f(S) = max(cost(S), min_{v ∈ S} f(S ∖ v))`, `cost(S)` the vertices of `S`\n    with a neighbour outside `S`. Exact; about 20 vertices in seconds.\"\"\"\n    n = len(adj)\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 0\n    N = 1 << n\n    states = np.arange(N, dtype=np.int64)\n    cost = np.zeros(N, dtype=np.int32)\n    pc = np.zeros(N, dtype=np.int32)\n    for u in range(n):\n        inside = (states >> u) & 1\n        pc += inside.astype(np.int32)\n        has_out = (np.int64(adj[u]) & ~states) != 0\n        cost += (inside & has_out).astype(np.int32)\n    cost[N - 1] = 0\n    f = np.full(N, INF, dtype=np.int32)\n    f[0] = 0\n    for k in range(1, n + 1):\n        S = states[pc == k]\n        best = np.full(len(S), INF, dtype=np.int32)\n        for v in range(n):\n            has = ((S >> v) & 1).astype(bool)\n            best = np.where(has, np.minimum(best, f[S ^ (1 << v)]), best)\n        f[S] = np.maximum(best, cost[S])\n    return int(f[N - 1])\n\n\ndef vs(g) -> int:\n    return vs_subset_dp(g[1])\n\n\ndef mosp_subset_dp(matrix) -> int:\n    \"\"\"MOSP value by DP over the set of produced patterns: the stacks open\n    after producing `S` are the customers with a pattern in `S` and one\n    outside; the value of an order is the max over its prefixes. Nothing\n    here mentions a graph or a layout.\"\"\"\n    rows = [sum(1 << j for j, x in enumerate(r) if x) for r in matrix]\n    m = len(matrix[0]) if matrix else 0\n    if m == 0:\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# the reductions\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef max_degree(g) -> int:\n    return max((bin(a).count(\"1\") for a in g[1]), default=0)\n\n\ndef is_cobipartite(g) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Some bipartition into two cliques (complement bipartite).\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    comp = [((1 << n) - 1) & ~adj[v] & ~(1 << v) for v in range(n)]\n    colour = [-1] * n\n    for s in range(n):\n        if colour[s] >= 0:\n            continue\n        colour[s] = 0\n        stack = [s]\n        while stack:\n            v = stack.pop()\n            bits = comp[v]\n            while bits:\n                u = (bits & -bits).bit_length() - 1\n                bits &= bits - 1\n                if colour[u] < 0:\n                    colour[u] = 1 - colour[v]\n                    stack.append(u)\n                elif colour[u] == colour[v]:\n                    return False\n    return True\n\n\ndef cobipartite_double(g):\n    \"\"\"`H`: cliques `A = {a_v}`, `B = {b_v}`; `a_u ~ b_v` iff `u = v` or `uv ∈ E`.\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    es = []\n    for u in range(n):\n        for v in range(u + 1, n):\n            es.append((u, v))\n            es.append((n + u, n + v))\n        es.append((u, n + u))\n        for v in range(n):\n            if adj[u] >> v & 1:\n                es.append((u, n + v))\n    return graph(2 * n, es)\n\n\ndef clique_blowup(g, c: int):\n    \"\"\"The lexicographic product `G[K_c]`: vertex `v` becomes a clique\n    `{cv, …, cv + c − 1}`, every edge a complete join between cliques.\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    es = []\n    for u in range(n):\n        for i in range(c):\n            for j in range(i + 1, c):\n                es.append((c * u + i, c * u + j))\n            for v in range(u + 1, n):\n                if adj[u] >> v & 1:\n                    for j in range(c):\n                        es.append((c * u + i, c * v + j))\n    return graph(c * n, es)\n\n\ndef degree_clique_expansion(g):\n    \"\"\"`G°`: a port per (vertex, incident edge); the ports of a vertex form a\n    clique; the two ports of an edge are joined. Isolated vertices stay.\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    es = edges_of(g)\n    port, cnt = {}, 0\n    for i, (u, v) in enumerate(es):\n        port[(u, i)] = cnt\n        port[(v, i)] = cnt + 1\n        cnt += 2\n    new = []\n    for u in range(n):\n        ps = [port[(u, i)] for i, e in enumerate(es) if u in e]\n        new += list(itertools.combinations(ps, 2))\n    for i, (u, v) in enumerate(es):\n        new.append((port[(u, i)], port[(v, i)]))\n    iso = sum(1 for u in range(n) if adj[u] == 0)\n    return graph(cnt + iso, new)\n\n\ndef mosp_instance(g):\n    \"\"\"One pattern per edge, one private pattern per isolated vertex; the\ndef atlas(nmax: int):\n    from networkx.generators.atlas import graph_atlas_g\n    return [from_nx(h) for h in graph_atlas_g() if 1 <= h.number_of_nodes() <= nmax]\n\n\ndef all_matrices(rows: int, cols: int):\n===== CLASSES\nfrom learning.subexp_reductions import atlas, vs\nfrom paper1.complex_check import graph, vertex_separation, vs_of_layout\n\nOUT = Path(\"reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/04_classes\")\nINF = 10 ** 9\n\n\ndef popcount(x: int) -> int:\n    return bin(x).count(\"1\")\n\n\ndef bits(x: int):\n    while x:\n        b = x & -x\n        yield b.bit_length() - 1\n        x ^= b\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# cobipartite graphs\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef is_clique(g, mask: int) -> bool:\n    n, adj = g\n    return all((adj[v] | (1 << v)) & mask == mask for v in bits(mask))\n\n\ndef is_independent(g, mask: int) -> bool:\n    n, adj = g\n    return all(adj[v] & mask == 0 for v in bits(mask))\n\n\ndef cobipartitions(g):\n    \"\"\"Every `(A, B)` with `A ∪ B = V`, both cliques, `A` containing vertex 0\n    (so each unordered partition once).\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    full = (1 << n) - 1\n    for A in range(1, 1 << n, 2):\n        B = full & ~A\n        if is_clique(g, A) and is_clique(g, B):\n            yield A, B\n\n\ndef chain_value(g, X: int, Y: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"`min_τ max_{0 ≤ t < |Y|} (t + |N_X(Y_{>t})|)` by a DP over suffix sets of\n    `Y`: `f(S) = max(cost(S), min_{v ∈ S} f(S ∖ v))`, `cost(S) = |Y| − |S| +\n    |N_X(S)|`, `f(∅) = 0`; the answer is `f(Y)`.\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    y = popcount(Y)\n    if y == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n    def f(S: int) -> int:\n        if S == 0:\n            return 0\n        nx = 0\n        for v in bits(S):\n            nx |= adj[v]\n        cost = y - popcount(S) + popcount(nx & X)\n        return max(cost, min(f(S & ~(1 << v)) for v in bits(S)))\n\n    return f(Y)\n\n\ndef cobipartite_normal_form(g, A: int, B: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"`vs(H)` by the normal form: `min_X max(|X| − 1, chain_X)`.\"\"\"\n    vals = []\n    for X, Y in ((A, B), (B, A)):\n        if X == 0:\n            continue\n        vals.append(max(popcount(X) - 1, chain_value(g, X, Y)))\n    return min(vals)\n\n\ndef cobipartite_decision(g, A: int, B: int, k: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Corollary 4.2 as stated: `vs(H) ≤ k` iff for `X = A` or `X = B`,\n    `|X| − 1 ≤ k` and some chain `∅ = S_0 ⊂ S_1 ⊂ … ⊂ S_|Y| = Y` has\n    `|N_X(S_s)| − s ≤ k − |Y|` for every `1 ≤ s ≤ |Y|` (`S_0` unconstrained).\n    Evaluated by a DP over subsets of `Y`.\"\"\"\n# twins and the unit DP\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef twin_classes(g) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Classes of the relation `x ~ y` iff `N(x) ∖ {y} = N(y) ∖ {x}` (true or\n    false twins; it is an equivalence: each class is a clique or an\n    independent set that is a module).\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    classes: list[int] = []\n    seen = 0\n    for x in range(n):\n        if seen >> x & 1:\n            continue\n        cls = 1 << x\n        for y in range(x + 1, n):\n            if seen >> y & 1:\n                continue\n            if adj[x] & ~(1 << y) == adj[y] & ~(1 << x):\n                cls |= 1 << y\n        seen |= cls\n        classes.append(cls)\n    return classes\n\n\ndef cut(g, placed: int) -> int:\n    n, adj = g\n    return sum(1 for v in bits(placed) if adj[v] & ~placed)\n\n\ndef block_cost(g, P: int, Q: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"The largest cut while the class `Q` is placed consecutively after the\n    prefix set `P` (any internal order: the members are twins).\"\"\"\n    best, cur = 0, P\n    for v in bits(Q):\n        cur |= 1 << v\n        best = max(best, cut(g, cur))\n    return best\n\n\ndef twin_unit_dp(g) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"`(vs, u)`: vertex separation by a DP over subsets of twin classes, and\n    the number of classes.\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 0, n\n    classes = twin_classes(g)\n    u = len(classes)\n\n    @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n    def f(S: int) -> int:        # S: subset of classes, as a mask over classes\n        if S == 0:\n            return 0\n        best = INF\n        for i in bits(S):\n            rest = S & ~(1 << i)\n            P = 0\n            for j in bits(rest):\n                P |= classes[j]\n            best = min(best, max(f(rest), block_cost(g, P, classes[i])))\n        return best\n\n    return f((1 << u) - 1), u\n\n\ndef consecutive_twin_layout_value(g) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Minimum over layouts in which every twin class is consecutive — the\n    literal form of the twin lemma.\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    classes = twin_classes(g)\n    best = INF\n===== CW\n    def cost(s, v):\n        t = s | (1 << v)\n        if t == full:\n            return 0\n        return sum(1 for u in range(n) if t >> u & 1 and adj[u] & ~t)\n\n    return _layout_dp(n, cost)\n\n\ndef cutwidth_of_layout(g, order):\n    \"\"\"Lengauer p. 468 (min-cut linear arrangement): edges crossing each gap.\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    best = 0\n    for pre in _prefix_masks(order)[:-1]:\n        c = sum(popcount(adj[u] & ~pre) for u in range(n) if pre >> u & 1)\n        best = max(best, c)\n    return best\n\n\ndef cutwidth(g):\n    n, _ = g\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 0\n    return min(cutwidth_of_layout(g, p) for p in itertools.permutations(range(n)))\n\n\ndef cutwidth_dp(g):\n    n, adj = g\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 0\n    full = (1 << n) - 1\n\n    def cost(s, v):\n        t = s | (1 << v)\n        if t == full:\n            return 0\n        return sum(popcount(adj[u] & ~t) for u in range(n) if t >> u & 1)\n\n    return _layout_dp(n, cost)\n\n\n===== MISSING\nKolesnik & Wormald 2014 is arXiv 1311.6555 (1109.0689 is a paper on payment\nsystems); Díaz–Diner–Serna–Serra's vertex bisection paper is published as\n*J. Comput. System Sci.* 2024, doi:10.1016/j.jcss.2024.103550.\n\n## The OpenAI mathematics collection (loop0014 item 02, 2026-11-04)\n\nNot missing — held locally, read-only, and recorded here because one file\nof it is now copied into this development.\n\n- **OpenAI** (2026). *The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False*, family 378\n  of the OpenAI mathematics collection (`github.com/openai/math`,\n  Apache-2.0), manuscript of 17 October 2026 revised 25 October 2026, 64\n  pages; the Lean comparator statement\n  `lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean` (theorem\n  `exact_count_and_sat`, proved in `lean/OAI/Algorithms/SubexponentialSAT/`).\n  Cloned read-only at `~/dev/untrusted/openai-math` (commit `4eaee875`,\n  2026-10-29); the paper is read as a `pdftotext` extract\n  (`reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/01_audit/eth.txt`, git-ignored) and\n  the Lean files with `cat`; nothing in the clone is built or run, and\n  nothing is imported from it. **Copied verbatim**, under its Apache-2.0\n  header: lines 1–190 and 220 of the comparator file (the definitions —\n  the CNF syntax, canonical encoding, `PrimitiveMachine` model, `Trace`,\n  located configurations, `runtimeScale`, `runtimeBound`; file SHA-256\n  `4183b2057cff350d66848215ff7167196dfc20e1891b598fdddea59077a76685`) into\n  `lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/PrimitiveMachine.lean`. The\n  theorem itself is **not** copied: it is restated as the named `Prop`\n  `SubexpCount` in `SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean` and never asserted\n  (`reports/subexp_pathwidth.md` §2). The verified exponent is\n  `n (log log n)² / √log n`; the paper's sharper exponents are paper-only\n  (§1.0).\n175:| 7 | Edge search game | Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1986 [10] | graph (multigraphs allowed) | itself | `ns − 1 ≤ es ≤ ns + 1` ([10] p. 209) ⇔ `vs ≤ es ≤ vs + 2` (Ellis, Sudborough & Turner 1994 Thm 2.1); all three values occur; `es(G) = vs(2-expansion of G)` (EST Thm 2.2) | 1,632 graphs; `es − vs ∈ {0,1,2}` | `Complex.vertexSeparation_le_progressiveEdgeSearch_le_add_two`, `pathwidth_le_progressiveEdgeSearch_le_add_two`, `edgeSearch_le_vertexSeparation_add_two`, `progressiveEdgeSearch_le_monotoneNodeSearch_add_one`, `monotoneNodeSearch_sub_one_le_progressiveEdgeSearch`, `edgeSearch_of_edgeless`; gap stated as `EdgeSearchMonotonicity`; item 14: `vertexSeparation_le_edgeSearch_le_add_two`, `pathwidth_le_edgeSearch_le_add_two`, `nodeSearch_sub_one_le_edgeSearch_le_add_one` | weaker than stated (band of width 2); **proved for the progressive game** (`vs ≤ s ≤ vs + 2`, every finite graph) and `s ≤ vs + 2` for the full game; `vs ≤ s` in the full game **proved by item 14** without LaPaugh; `s = ps` (LaPaugh 1993) remains the named gap |\n407:  EST Thm 2.2, `s(G) = vs(G')` with G' the 2-expansion (each edge subdivided\n584:   vs(2-expansion)`; `ns − 1 ≤ es ≤ ns + 1`.\n623:minimum. `vs_dp` is used only on the constructed graphs (2-expansions,\n631:the 2-expansion check to `n + 2m ≤ 18` (181), `G_du` to `n + m ≤ 16` (680).\n648:| 6 | `es = vs(2-expansion)` | 181 | 0 |\n1231:which needs a lower bound over all strategies) and EST Thm 2.2 (`s(G) = vs(2-expansion)`); both stay","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"09aaa989-9eda-444c-a4fd-1cc6d69ace0c","uuid":"f190a625-1cd4-4fe2-958a-a1621c1b0e1f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T00:12:27.648Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"23:undirected graph in the same amount of time, improving on the algorithm due to\n31:The pathwidth [2,21] of an undirected graph G is defined as follows. A path-\n62:The directed pathwidth of G is defined similarly to the pathwidth of an undirected\n65:    For an undirected graph G, let Ĝ denote the digraph obtained from G by replacing\n70:pathwidth of an undirected graph is a special case of the problem of computing the\n95:    Without parameterization, both directed and undirected pathwidth can be solved in\n98:problems (see [4]). Similarly, some undirected graph parameters related to pathwidth,\n132:    A key to overcoming this difficulty is the following observation whose undirected\n215:[26] (see also [15] for the undirected case). Based on this fact, we work on the directed\n274:   Following the terminology of [24] for the undirected case, we call U ⊆ V (G) a full\n319:tions of the lemma, U ∪ C is strongly k-feasible if U is. The undirected counterpart\n=====\nproblems in the works of Esmer et al. [15, 16, 17]. Even more recently, Inamdar et al. [26] gave\na framework for designing exponential-time approximation schemes for graph partitioning (i.e.,\ncut) problems and a few more examples for specific problems are known [8, 9, 4]. However, none\nof these techniques seem applicable to vertex-ordering problems, where the objective is to find an\nordering of vertices that satisfies some polynomial-time satisfiable predicate, such as Feedback\nArc Set, Optimal Linear Arrangement, Cutwidth, and Pathwidth. For all of the above\nexcept Pathwidth, the O∗ (2n )-time dynamic programming remains the best known algorithm–\neven including constant-factor approximations. Moreover, the question whether e.g., Feedback\nArc Set or Cutwidth can be solved faster than O∗ (2n ) have been explicit open problems for\na long time [21, 24, 25, 13]. This reinforces our belief that it is time to study exponential-time\napproximation algorithms for vertex-ordering problems. The only example of such a study is in\n===== CODE\n\nOUT = Path(\"reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/03_reductions\")\nINF = 10 ** 9\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# exact evaluators\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef vs_subset_dp(adj) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Vertex separation by DP over prefix sets, vectorised by popcount layer:\n    `f(S) = max(cost(S), min_{v ∈ S} f(S ∖ v))`, `cost(S)` the vertices of `S`\n    with a neighbour outside `S`. Exact; about 20 vertices in seconds.\"\"\"\n    n = len(adj)\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 0\n    N = 1 << n\n    states = np.arange(N, dtype=np.int64)\n    cost = np.zeros(N, dtype=np.int32)\n    pc = np.zeros(N, dtype=np.int32)\n    for u in range(n):\n        inside = (states >> u) & 1\n        pc += inside.astype(np.int32)\n        has_out = (np.int64(adj[u]) & ~states) != 0\n        cost += (inside & has_out).astype(np.int32)\n    cost[N - 1] = 0\n    f = np.full(N, INF, dtype=np.int32)\n    f[0] = 0\n    for k in range(1, n + 1):\n        S = states[pc == k]\n        best = np.full(len(S), INF, dtype=np.int32)\n        for v in range(n):\n            has = ((S >> v) & 1).astype(bool)\n            best = np.where(has, np.minimum(best, f[S ^ (1 << v)]), best)\n        f[S] = np.maximum(best, cost[S])\n    return int(f[N - 1])\n\n\ndef vs(g) -> int:\n    return vs_subset_dp(g[1])\n\n\ndef mosp_subset_dp(matrix) -> int:\n    \"\"\"MOSP value by DP over the set of produced patterns: the stacks open\n    after producing `S` are the customers with a pattern in `S` and one\n    outside; the value of an order is the max over its prefixes. Nothing\n    here mentions a graph or a layout.\"\"\"\n    rows = [sum(1 << j for j, x in enumerate(r) if x) for r in matrix]\n    m = len(matrix[0]) if matrix else 0\n    if m == 0:\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# the reductions\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef max_degree(g) -> int:\n    return max((bin(a).count(\"1\") for a in g[1]), default=0)\n\n\ndef is_cobipartite(g) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Some bipartition into two cliques (complement bipartite).\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    comp = [((1 << n) - 1) & ~adj[v] & ~(1 << v) for v in range(n)]\n    colour = [-1] * n\n    for s in range(n):\n        if colour[s] >= 0:\n            continue\n        colour[s] = 0\n        stack = [s]\n        while stack:\n            v = stack.pop()\n            bits = comp[v]\n            while bits:\n                u = (bits & -bits).bit_length() - 1\n                bits &= bits - 1\n                if colour[u] < 0:\n                    colour[u] = 1 - colour[v]\n                    stack.append(u)\n                elif colour[u] == colour[v]:\n                    return False\n    return True\n\n\ndef cobipartite_double(g):\n    \"\"\"`H`: cliques `A = {a_v}`, `B = {b_v}`; `a_u ~ b_v` iff `u = v` or `uv ∈ E`.\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    es = []\n    for u in range(n):\n        for v in range(u + 1, n):\n            es.append((u, v))\n            es.append((n + u, n + v))\n        es.append((u, n + u))\n        for v in range(n):\n            if adj[u] >> v & 1:\n                es.append((u, n + v))\n    return graph(2 * n, es)\n\n\ndef clique_blowup(g, c: int):\n    \"\"\"The lexicographic product `G[K_c]`: vertex `v` becomes a clique\n    `{cv, …, cv + c − 1}`, every edge a complete join between cliques.\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    es = []\n    for u in range(n):\n        for i in range(c):\n            for j in range(i + 1, c):\n                es.append((c * u + i, c * u + j))\n            for v in range(u + 1, n):\n                if adj[u] >> v & 1:\n                    for j in range(c):\n                        es.append((c * u + i, c * v + j))\n    return graph(c * n, es)\n\n\ndef degree_clique_expansion(g):\n    \"\"\"`G°`: a port per (vertex, incident edge); the ports of a vertex form a\n    clique; the two ports of an edge are joined. Isolated vertices stay.\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    es = edges_of(g)\n    port, cnt = {}, 0\n    for i, (u, v) in enumerate(es):\n        port[(u, i)] = cnt\n        port[(v, i)] = cnt + 1\n        cnt += 2\n    new = []\n    for u in range(n):\n        ps = [port[(u, i)] for i, e in enumerate(es) if u in e]\n        new += list(itertools.combinations(ps, 2))\n    for i, (u, v) in enumerate(es):\n        new.append((port[(u, i)], port[(v, i)]))\n    iso = sum(1 for u in range(n) if adj[u] == 0)\n    return graph(cnt + iso, new)\n\n\ndef mosp_instance(g):\n    \"\"\"One pattern per edge, one private pattern per isolated vertex; the\ndef atlas(nmax: int):\n    from networkx.generators.atlas import graph_atlas_g\n    return [from_nx(h) for h in graph_atlas_g() if 1 <= h.number_of_nodes() <= nmax]\n\n\ndef all_matrices(rows: int, cols: int):\n===== CLASSES\nfrom learning.subexp_reductions import atlas, vs\nfrom paper1.complex_check import graph, vertex_separation, vs_of_layout\n\nOUT = Path(\"reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/04_classes\")\nINF = 10 ** 9\n\n\ndef popcount(x: int) -> int:\n    return bin(x).count(\"1\")\n\n\ndef bits(x: int):\n    while x:\n        b = x & -x\n        yield b.bit_length() - 1\n        x ^= b\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# cobipartite graphs\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef is_clique(g, mask: int) -> bool:\n    n, adj = g\n    return all((adj[v] | (1 << v)) & mask == mask for v in bits(mask))\n\n\ndef is_independent(g, mask: int) -> bool:\n    n, adj = g\n    return all(adj[v] & mask == 0 for v in bits(mask))\n\n\ndef cobipartitions(g):\n    \"\"\"Every `(A, B)` with `A ∪ B = V`, both cliques, `A` containing vertex 0\n    (so each unordered partition once).\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    full = (1 << n) - 1\n    for A in range(1, 1 << n, 2):\n        B = full & ~A\n        if is_clique(g, A) and is_clique(g, B):\n            yield A, B\n\n\ndef chain_value(g, X: int, Y: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"`min_τ max_{0 ≤ t < |Y|} (t + |N_X(Y_{>t})|)` by a DP over suffix sets of\n    `Y`: `f(S) = max(cost(S), min_{v ∈ S} f(S ∖ v))`, `cost(S) = |Y| − |S| +\n    |N_X(S)|`, `f(∅) = 0`; the answer is `f(Y)`.\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    y = popcount(Y)\n    if y == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n    def f(S: int) -> int:\n        if S == 0:\n            return 0\n        nx = 0\n        for v in bits(S):\n            nx |= adj[v]\n        cost = y - popcount(S) + popcount(nx & X)\n        return max(cost, min(f(S & ~(1 << v)) for v in bits(S)))\n\n    return f(Y)\n\n\ndef cobipartite_normal_form(g, A: int, B: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"`vs(H)` by the normal form: `min_X max(|X| − 1, chain_X)`.\"\"\"\n    vals = []\n    for X, Y in ((A, B), (B, A)):\n        if X == 0:\n            continue\n        vals.append(max(popcount(X) - 1, chain_value(g, X, Y)))\n    return min(vals)\n\n\ndef cobipartite_decision(g, A: int, B: int, k: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Corollary 4.2 as stated: `vs(H) ≤ k` iff for `X = A` or `X = B`,\n    `|X| − 1 ≤ k` and some chain `∅ = S_0 ⊂ S_1 ⊂ … ⊂ S_|Y| = Y` has\n    `|N_X(S_s)| − s ≤ k − |Y|` for every `1 ≤ s ≤ |Y|` (`S_0` unconstrained).\n    Evaluated by a DP over subsets of `Y`.\"\"\"\n# twins and the unit DP\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef twin_classes(g) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Classes of the relation `x ~ y` iff `N(x) ∖ {y} = N(y) ∖ {x}` (true or\n    false twins; it is an equivalence: each class is a clique or an\n    independent set that is a module).\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    classes: list[int] = []\n    seen = 0\n    for x in range(n):\n        if seen >> x & 1:\n            continue\n        cls = 1 << x\n        for y in range(x + 1, n):\n            if seen >> y & 1:\n                continue\n            if adj[x] & ~(1 << y) == adj[y] & ~(1 << x):\n                cls |= 1 << y\n        seen |= cls\n        classes.append(cls)\n    return classes\n\n\ndef cut(g, placed: int) -> int:\n    n, adj = g\n    return sum(1 for v in bits(placed) if adj[v] & ~placed)\n\n\ndef block_cost(g, P: int, Q: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"The largest cut while the class `Q` is placed consecutively after the\n    prefix set `P` (any internal order: the members are twins).\"\"\"\n    best, cur = 0, P\n    for v in bits(Q):\n        cur |= 1 << v\n        best = max(best, cut(g, cur))\n    return best\n\n\ndef twin_unit_dp(g) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"`(vs, u)`: vertex separation by a DP over subsets of twin classes, and\n    the number of classes.\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 0, n\n    classes = twin_classes(g)\n    u = len(classes)\n\n    @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n    def f(S: int) -> int:        # S: subset of classes, as a mask over classes\n        if S == 0:\n            return 0\n        best = INF\n        for i in bits(S):\n            rest = S & ~(1 << i)\n            P = 0\n            for j in bits(rest):\n                P |= classes[j]\n            best = min(best, max(f(rest), block_cost(g, P, classes[i])))\n        return best\n\n    return f((1 << u) - 1), u\n\n\ndef consecutive_twin_layout_value(g) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Minimum over layouts in which every twin class is consecutive — the\n    literal form of the twin lemma.\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    classes = twin_classes(g)\n    best = INF\n===== CW\n    def cost(s, v):\n        t = s | (1 << v)\n        if t == full:\n            return 0\n        return sum(1 for u in range(n) if t >> u & 1 and adj[u] & ~t)\n\n    return _layout_dp(n, cost)\n\n\ndef cutwidth_of_layout(g, order):\n    \"\"\"Lengauer p. 468 (min-cut linear arrangement): edges crossing each gap.\"\"\"\n    n, adj = g\n    best = 0\n    for pre in _prefix_masks(order)[:-1]:\n        c = sum(popcount(adj[u] & ~pre) for u in range(n) if pre >> u & 1)\n        best = max(best, c)\n    return best\n\n\ndef cutwidth(g):\n    n, _ = g\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 0\n    return min(cutwidth_of_layout(g, p) for p in itertools.permutations(range(n)))\n\n\ndef cutwidth_dp(g):\n    n, adj = g\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 0\n    full = (1 << n) - 1\n\n    def cost(s, v):\n        t = s | (1 << v)\n        if t == full:\n            return 0\n        return sum(popcount(adj[u] & ~t) for u in range(n) if t >> u & 1)\n\n    return _layout_dp(n, cost)\n\n\n===== MISSING\nKolesnik & Wormald 2014 is arXiv 1311.6555 (1109.0689 is a paper on payment\nsystems); Díaz–Diner–Serna–Serra's vertex bisection paper is published as\n*J. Comput. System Sci.* 2024, doi:10.1016/j.jcss.2024.103550.\n\n## The OpenAI mathematics collection (loop0014 item 02, 2026-11-04)\n\nNot missing — held locally, read-only, and recorded here because one file\nof it is now copied into this development.\n\n- **OpenAI** (2026). *The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False*, family 378\n  of the OpenAI mathematics collection (`github.com/openai/math`,\n  Apache-2.0), manuscript of 17 October 2026 revised 25 October 2026, 64\n  pages; the Lean comparator statement\n  `lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean` (theorem\n  `exact_count_and_sat`, proved in `lean/OAI/Algorithms/SubexponentialSAT/`).\n  Cloned read-only at `~/dev/untrusted/openai-math` (commit `4eaee875`,\n  2026-10-29); the paper is read as a `pdftotext` extract\n  (`reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/01_audit/eth.txt`, git-ignored) and\n  the Lean files with `cat`; nothing in the clone is built or run, and\n  nothing is imported from it. **Copied verbatim**, under its Apache-2.0\n  header: lines 1–190 and 220 of the comparator file (the definitions —\n  the CNF syntax, canonical encoding, `PrimitiveMachine` model, `Trace`,\n  located configur