{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgirPJBgKWRwT8R2uJTqK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 07 is complete: §7 written, code and tests in place, checks clean, the refuter's points folded in, the gate passed, PROGRESS.md and iterations.md updated. Not committed, nothing under `solutions/` touched, no Lean file changed.\n\n**What the item produced**\n\n- **Theorem 7.1** (`reports/subexp_pathwidth.md` §7.1): the line graph carries cutwidth to vertex separation. For every graph without a single-edge component, cutwidth is at most the vertex separation of the line graph, and the reverse inequality holds for every graph up to an excess of ⌊Δ/2⌋ − 1, attained by stars. Equality for maximum degree 3. The line graph has m vertices against the 2m ports of item 03's expansion, so the transfer's exponent halves. Both layout maps are explicit; the proof of the forward map needed a tie rule that the first draft got wrong, caught by the check before it was stated.\n- **Algorithm 7.2**, cutwidth of subcubic graphs with a witness: O*(2^m) as implemented, O(1.89^m) with Kitsunai et al. The honest base per vertex is 1.89^(m/n), which is 2.598 on cubic graphs, so the transfer does not give a base below 2 on cubic graphs. It does below m < 1.0888 n, a regime I do not claim is interesting. Bentert et al.'s open problem stands.\n- **The converse** (vertex separation as a cutwidth) was explored on seven candidate transformations over all 253 subcubic atlas graphs and not found. The obstacle is stated with its smallest example, and §7.0 explains why the converse would not have carried the base anyway.\n- **Algorithms 7.3 to 7.6**: item 04's cobipartite, twin-class, MOSP equivalent-customer and starlike chordal algorithms, each with a proof, a witness and code in `learning/subexp_algorithms.py`. The starlike one is Gustedt's Algorithm 6.3 implemented from the held paper, giving Corollary 4.5 as an algorithm in 2^(2√n) time.\n- **Edge search on subcubic graphs** equals cutwidth on all 153 atlas graphs where it could be computed. That is recorded as checked and attributed to Makedon & Sudborough, not proved and in no theorem.\n\n**Verification.** The full check over every atlas graph to 7 vertices, 2,592 starlike graphs, 496 cobipartitions and random instances reported zero failures in every table, in 91 s. One refuter subagent read the proofs blind with its own brute force and found no wrong statement, only three editorial points, now folded in. The gate passed: lake build clean, no sorry or axiom, 1,978 tests passed, the solver's 123 passed, paper check clean. After the gate I changed only one docstring sentence, and the item's ten tests re-ran green.\n\n**Next** is item 08, which formalises Theorem 7.1 (with the tie rule and the single-edge exception as part of the statement), Theorem 4.1 and Theorem 4.4 in Lean."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5144,"cache_read_input_tokens":276513,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5144,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"09aaa989-9eda-444c-a4fd-1cc6d69ace0c","uuid":"95af849b-abff-4c58-bcde-8258fc1df58c","timestamp":"2026-11-05T00:42:06.609Z","request_id":"req_011CgirPHqLh5UY2dAnUc5hy"}
